.lua
book.chapter(17, "Signatures and abstraction")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A signature states the public contract"
.pp
A module signature lists the types and values a structure must provide.
Define a named signature with module type Name := sig ... end. Value
specifications use val name : type. An abstract type declaration omits a
representation, while a manifest alias deliberately exposes an equality.
These choices determine what clients may assume, not just how much
documentation appears beside an implementation.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[module type COUNTER := sig
  type t
  val zero : t
  val bump : t -> t
  val get : t -> Int
end
module Hidden_counter :> COUNTER := struct
  type t = Int
  val zero := 0
  val bump n := n + 1
  val get n := n
  val implementation_detail := 99
end
Test.equal "abstract client" (Hidden_counter.get
  (Hidden_counter.bump Hidden_counter.zero)) 1]=], "An opaque counter interface")
.endlua
.pp
The client can obtain a counter, increment it, and inspect its numeric value.
It cannot rely on the hidden representation being Int or on the private
implementation_detail member. That freedom lets the module later use a
record, a constructor, or another representation while preserving the
documented operations. An interface is valuable precisely because it limits
which implementation facts become dependencies.
.sh 2 "Transparent and opaque ascription"
.pp
A single colon introduces transparent ascription. The :> spelling seals
opaquely. Both check compatibility with the signature. In the current
checker, transparent ascription retains the implementation scope; it should
not be used as a promise to hide extra members. Opaque sealing restricts the
visible surface and gives abstract types a public identity independent of
their implementation representation.
.pp
This difference is easy to miss if every example happens to use only the
declared members. Test abstraction with a negative client as well as a
successful one. A counter library is not properly protected merely because
Counter.get works; a client that tries to pass an arbitrary integer where
Counter.t is required should also be rejected under an opaque interface.
The negative test checks the boundary that makes future changes safe.
.lua
book.code("metaml-error:M0502", [=[module type REQUIRED := sig
  val count : Int
end
module Missing :> REQUIRED := struct
  val other := 1
end]=], "A missing required member violates the signature")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Abstract identity versus exposed equality"
.pp
An abstract type says that clients know there is a type but do not know its
representation. A manifest specification such as type t = Int intentionally
shares the integer type. Use the latter when callers must interoperate
directly with integers; use the former when operations should preserve a
module-owned invariant. Neither choice is universally better. The right
choice follows the intended client behavior.
.pp
Consider a validated count. Exposing Int allows any integer to be passed,
including a negative one. Hiding the representation and exposing a checked
constructor can ensure that ordinary clients only receive valid values.
The signature must then expose enough observations and transformations to
make the abstraction useful. An opaque type with no way to construct or
inspect values is technically hidden but rarely a productive API.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[module type COUNT := sig
  type t
  val make : Int -> Option t
  val value : t -> Int
end
module Count :> COUNT := struct
  type t = Int
  val make n := if n < 0 then None else Some n
  val value n := n
end
Test.equal "reject invalid" (Count.make (~1) |> Option.map Count.value) None
Test.equal "accept valid" (Count.make 7 |> Option.map Count.value) (Some 7)]=],
    "A checked constructor establishes a semantic invariant")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Polymorphism is part of compatibility"
.pp
A matching value name is not enough. The implementation's type must satisfy
the specification, including type-constructor arguments and polymorphism.
A function specialized to Int cannot stand in for a promised identity over
every type. Conversely, a sufficiently general implementation can often
serve a narrower advertised contract. Think of the signature as the set of
calls clients are entitled to make, and check that the implementation can
honor all of them.
.lua
book.algorithm("Review a module interface", {
    "List the values clients need and the type relationships between them.",
    "Choose which type equalities are public and which are abstract.",
    "Check every required member against its full specified type.",
    "Seal the implementation when private members must remain inaccessible.",
    "Test a legitimate client and an attempted representation leak.",
    "Keep implementation helpers outside the public signature."
})
.endlua
.pp
The standard library uses sealed modules to keep helper definitions private.
An accumulator loop, a host primitive wrapper, or an internal conversion can
change without expanding the public surface. This also makes generated API
reference material more useful: a signature is a curated contract rather
than a dump of every temporary name in the source file. When documenting a
new library, explain the invariants behind the types as well as the types.
.sh 2 "Abstraction across multiple implementations"
.pp
A signature can support several implementations, such as a simple list-based
collection and a specialized native-backed collection. Clients should depend
on specified behavior, including ordering and failure semantics, rather than
only on function types. Two modules can have identical type signatures but
disagree about duplicate handling, stable enumeration, or error conditions.
Those semantic differences must be tested and documented separately.
.pp
This observation also applies to MetaML's C++ and D dialects. Their native
representations differ, but the language-facing contracts must agree. A
signature helps describe that boundary; parity tests verify observable
behavior across it. Abstraction therefore supports portability without
pretending that one type declaration proves every implementation property.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read signature parsing, module compatibility checks, and opaque type identity
handling in MetaML.hpp and MetaML.d. Study the sealed interfaces in
metalib/option/mod.ml and metalib/list/mod.ml. Design a nonempty-list module
with a checked constructor, head, and conversion back to a list. Decide
whether tail should return another nonempty value or an ordinary list, and
explain that choice through its type. Include a rejected construction case
alongside successful clients.
